@startuml
' Sources: SIDIS assignment replication requirement and LETI-SIDIS-PL3:
' local lookup first, at least two instances, independent stores, HTTP GET,
' circular-forwarding protection, timeouts and graceful error handling.
' Example path comes from PSOFT HangarController; forwarding is not implemented.
' PL3's simplified example returns 404 when all peers fail. Final error policy
' for unavailability is left TBD rather than claiming verified absence.
title AISafe - Peer-to-peer GET Forwarding\nAircraft Management example
skinparam monochrome true
skinparam shadowing false
skinparam defaultFontName SansSerif
skinparam defaultFontSize 13
skinparam ArrowFontSize 12
skinparam NoteBackgroundColor white
skinparam sequenceMessageAlign center
hide footbox
autonumber

actor "Client" as Client
participant "Service Replica A" as A
database "Local Store A" as StoreA
participant "Service Replica B" as B
database "Local Store B" as StoreB

Client -> A : GET /api/hangar/aircraft/{registrationNumber}
activate A
A -> StoreA : Find aircraft by registrationNumber
StoreA --> A : Local lookup result

alt A - Data found locally
  A --> Client : HTTP 200 OK\nLocal aircraft data
else B / C - Data not found locally
  A -> B : HTTP GET - same resource\nForwarding control: mechanism TBD
  alt B - Peer reachable
    B -> StoreB : Find aircraft by registrationNumber
    StoreB --> B : Peer lookup result
    alt B - Data found in peer
      B --> A : HTTP 200 OK\nAircraft data
      A --> Client : HTTP 200 OK\nAircraft data returned by peer
    else C - Data also absent in peer
      B --> A : HTTP 404 Not Found
      A --> Client : HTTP 404 Not Found
    end
  else C - Peer unavailable or timeout
    note over A, B
      No successful peer response within the timeout.
      Handle failure without indefinite blocking.
    end note
    A --> Client : Graceful failure response\nHTTP status policy: TBD
  end
end
deactivate A

legend bottom left
  Prevent circular forwarding; exclude self and track request hops/visited peers
  if needed. Header names, limits and timeout values are TBD.
  PL3 illustrates a 404 fallback even when a peer is down;
  the final policy must distinguish missing data from unavailability.
  This example retrieves one resource. Collection queries may aggregate
  local and peer results into a coherent response, handling duplicates.
  GET forwarding alone does not replicate writes or ensure eventual consistency.
endlegend
@enduml
